.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library is the controller side of a DescriptorQueue, the
.Xr dq 4
device.
A controller opens a queue, mints descriptors that present an identity of
its choosing, reads the events the kernel posts for operations on those
descriptors and answers them by id.
Every function is a thin wrapper over the
.Xr ioctl 2
and
.Xr read 2
interface of
.In dev/dq/dq.h ;
the kernel's errnos pass through unchanged and are listed below per
function.
Functions return 0 on success and -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure, except those documented to return a descriptor, a count or
a pointer.
The interface is LP64 only.
.Ss Queue
.Fn dq_open
opens
.Pa /dev/dq
with
.Dv O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC | O_CLOFORK
and returns the queue descriptor.
One open is one queue, owned by the calling process: the descriptor is in
no child and no exec'd image and cannot be sent, whatever its flags say,
only the calling process operates the queue, and the queue dies when that
process exits or execs
.Pq see Xr dq 4 , Sx The queue descriptor and its owner .
A child the controller forks inherits the minted descriptors and never the
queue; a process whose descriptor table holds the queue is refused
operations on the queue's objects with
.Er EDEADLK .
Opening fails with
.Er ENOENT
when the device is not configured,
.Er EPERM
or
.Er EACCES
without the
.Dv PRIV_DRIVER
privilege, and
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
from a 32-bit process.
.Pp
.Fn dq_get_stats
fills
.Fa st
with the
.Vt struct dq_stats
of the queue, or of the queue an observer descriptor
.Fa fd
watches.
.Fn dq_get_stats2
fills the extended
.Vt struct dq_stats2
.Pq Dv DQ_IOC_GET_STATS2 :
the same counters plus
.Va ds2_locks ,
.Va ds2_pushes ,
.Va ds2_bad_replies
(replies whose errno the operation's allow-list refused, which the
requester saw as
.Er EIO )
,
.Va ds2_overdepth ,
the page counters
.Va ds2_async_pending ,
.Va ds2_async_bytes ,
.Va ds2_async_deadlines
and
.Va ds2_close_dropped
.Pq Xr dq 4 ;
the kernel writes
.Va ds2_size .
.Pp
.Fn dq_set_queue_depth
lowers the number of unread events the queue holds;
.Fa depth
must be between 1 and
.Va kern.dq.queue_depth
or the call fails with
.Er EINVAL .
A full queue fails the requester's operation with
.Er EIO ,
counts a drop and leaves a gap in
.Va de_seq .
.Ss Identity and policy
.Fn dq_policy_default
fills
.Fa pol
with the default policy of
.Xr dq 4 ,
a read-only object:
.Va dp_cache
.Dv DQ_CACHE_NONE
(every read posts; pages exist only while mapped; whether
.Xr fstat 2
posts is decided by
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR
alone),
.Va dp_write ,
.Va dp_exec ,
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write
and
.Va dp_ioctl
.Dv DQ_DENY ,
.Va dp_lock
.Dv DQ_EVENT ,
.Va dp_deadline_ms
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT ,
no
.Va dp_events ,
.Va dp_rights_version
.Dv CAP_RIGHTS_VERSION
and
.Va dp_rights
.Dv CAP_ALL_RIGHTS ,
which is what
.Xr open 2
returns.
A controller narrows the rights, adds
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR
and
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_CLOSE ,
sets
.Va dp_write
to
.Dv DQ_EVENT
to serve writes and
.Va dp_ioctl
to
.Dv DQ_EVENT
to serve ioctls before minting.
Setting
.Va dp_cache
to
.Dv DQ_CACHE_DATA
caches read data in the kernel, and
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write
to
.Dv DQ_ALLOW
(with
.Va dp_write
.Dv DQ_EVENT )
allows shared writable mappings, whose dirty pages come back as
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC .
.Pp
.Fn dq_ident_from_stat
fills every field of
.Fa ident
from a real file:
.Fa path
is copied as presented (it must be absolute and normalized, as
.Xr realpath 3
returns it, or the mint fails with
.Dv DQ_REASON_PATH ) ,
the
.Vt struct stat
and
.Vt struct statfs
the caller took supply the attributes and the mount, and
.Xr fpathconf 2
is asked on
.Fa fd
for every
.Dv _PC_*
name in
.Va dq_pc_names[] ;
a name the filesystem refuses is left out of
.Va dp_valid
so the served descriptor refuses it with
.Er EINVAL
too.
.Fa fd
may be -1 to present no
.Xr pathconf 2
answers.
.Va di_blksize
is floored at the page size, which the kernel requires.
It fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a null or empty path and
.Er ENAMETOOLONG
for a path of
.Dv DQ_PATH_MAX
bytes or more.
.Ss Objects
.Fn dq_mint_file
mints a
.Dv DQ_KIND_FILE
object presenting
.Fa ident
under
.Fa pol .
On success
.Fa *object
is the object id, never reused on the queue, and
.Fa *fd
the descriptor installed in the caller:
read-only, close-on-exec, limited to
.Va dp_rights ,
and not transferable over
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
.Pq Er ENOTCAPABLE .
Until the resolution stage installs served descriptors in a requester, the
descriptor reaches one by inheritance across
.Xr fork 2 .
The
.Fa reason
pointer, which may be
.Dv NULL ,
is handed to the kernel as
.Va dm_reasonp
(a failing
.Xr ioctl 2
copies its argument back to nobody): on
.Er EINVAL
the kernel stores the refused field there as an
.Vt enum dq_reason
.Pq Xr dq 4 ,
on success
.Dv DQ_REASON_NONE ,
and on any other failure the library has already stored
.Dv DQ_REASON_NONE .
Other errors are
.Er ENOSPC
when the queue holds
.Va kern.dq.max_objects
objects,
.Er ENXIO
when the queue is closing, and those of
.Xr open 2
for the descriptor table.
.Pp
.Fn dq_mint_file_flags
is
.Fn dq_mint_file
with
.Va dm_flags :
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE
installs the descriptor readable and writable, as
.Xr open 2
with
.Dv O_RDWR
would, and needs a policy with
.Va dp_write
.Dv DQ_EVENT ;
without it, or with any other bit, the call fails with
.Er EINVAL
and
.Dv DQ_REASON_FLAGS .
.Fn dq_mint_file
mints read-only and remains for callers that predate the flag.
.Pp
.Fn dq_mint_observer
mints an observer descriptor of the queue and returns it.
It receives a copy of every event and every reply posted on the queue.
Without
.Dv DQ_OBS_F_DATA
data is removed and lengths kept: a
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
copy is the header alone and a
.Dv DQ_OP_REPLY
record the header and its
.Vt struct dq_reply_record ,
each flagged
.Dv DQ_EVENT_F_NODATA
when bytes were removed.
With it a write copy carries its bytes and a reply record is followed by
the reply's bytes padded to 8
.Pq Fn dq_event_reply_record .
The descriptor is readable, pollable, answers
.Dv DQ_IOC_GET_STATS
and
.Dv DQ_IOC_GET_STATS2
.Pq Fn dq_get_stats , Fn dq_get_stats2
and nothing else, and is locked close-on-exec, close-on-fork and
non-transferable.
.Er EINVAL
for an unknown flag,
.Er ENXIO
for a closing queue.
.Pp
.Fn dq_revoke
revokes
.Fa object :
every pending and in-flight event of it fails with
.Er EIO ,
.Dv NOTE_REVOKE
fires, later reads answer
.Er EIO ,
and
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2
and
.Dv F_KINFO
keep answering from the frozen identity.
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown id.
.Pp
.Fn dq_set_ident
replaces the presented identity after validation and fires
.Dv NOTE_ATTRIB
on the object's
.Dv EVFILT_VNODE
knotes.
.Er EINVAL
for an identity the kernel refuses (no reason is returned),
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
.Pp
.Fn dq_set_policy
changes
.Va dp_cache ,
.Va dp_ioctl ,
.Va dp_deadline_ms
and
.Va dp_events
of a minted object.
The fields the installed descriptor fixes,
.Va dp_write ,
.Va dp_exec ,
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write ,
.Va dp_lock
and
.Va dp_rights ,
must equal the minted values or the call fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
so does a policy outside the accepted set.
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
.Pp
.Fn dq_push_vnode_note
raises
.Fa note
on the object's
.Dv EVFILT_VNODE
knotes
.Pq Dv DQ_IOC_PUSH , DQ_PUSH_NOTIFY_VNODE
for what happened outside the requester's view: any combination of
.Dv NOTE_DELETE , NOTE_WRITE , NOTE_EXTEND , NOTE_ATTRIB , NOTE_LINK ,
.Dv NOTE_RENAME , NOTE_OPEN , NOTE_CLOSE , NOTE_CLOSE_WRITE
and
.Dv NOTE_READ .
.Er EINVAL
for 0, for
.Dv NOTE_REVOKE
.Pq use Fn dq_revoke
and for an unknown bit,
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
The kernel raises the notes of the object's own operations itself, and
.Dv NOTE_OPEN
and
.Dv NOTE_CLOSE
on the other objects of the same identity on the queue
.Pq Xr dq 4 .
.Pp
.Fn dq_push_invalidate
drops the clean cached pages of
.Fa object
in
.Bq Fa offset , offset No + Fa len
(rounded out to pages;
.Fa len
0 to the end), so the next fault on a mapping, or the next
.Dv DQ_CACHE_DATA
read, posts
.Dv DQ_OP_READ
again
.Pq Dv DQ_IOC_PUSH , DQ_PUSH_INVALIDATE ;
a controller calls it after changing the bytes it serves.
Dirty pages of a shared mapping it has not yet seen survive and are
written back.
.Er EINVAL
for a range past
.Dv OFF_MAX ,
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
.Ss Events
.Fn dq_read_events
performs one
.Xr read 2
of whole records into
.Fa buf
and stores the byte count in
.Fa *got .
The kernel copies as many whole records as fit, in order, and never splits
one;
.Fa len
below the size of a
.Vt struct dq_event
is
.Er EINVAL ,
a first record that does not fit is
.Er ENOBUFS ,
an empty queue opened or set non-blocking is
.Er EAGAIN ,
a closing queue is
.Er ENXIO ,
and a signal is
.Er EINTR .
A fault in
.Fa buf
after some records were copied returns those and leaves the rest queued.
On an observer descriptor a count of 0 means the watched queue is gone and
drained.
.Pp
.Fn dq_event_first
and
.Fn dq_event_next
walk the records in a buffer of
.Fa len
bytes.
Each returns
.Dv NULL
when no further whole record follows, that is when fewer than a header's
bytes remain or
.Va de_size
is smaller than a header, not a multiple of 8, or extends past
.Fa len .
.Fn dq_event_body
returns the body that follows the header and stores its length in
.Fa *bodylen
when that is not
.Dv NULL ;
the length includes the padding of a variable payload.
.Pp
The typed accessors
.Fn dq_event_read ,
.Fn dq_event_write ,
.Fn dq_event_setattr ,
.Fn dq_event_fsync ,
.Fn dq_event_fallocate ,
.Fn dq_event_fspacectl ,
.Fn dq_event_lock ,
.Fn dq_event_unlock_all ,
.Fn dq_event_ioctl
and
.Fn dq_event_interrupt
check that the record is of their operation and holds its fixed body, and
point
.Fa *body
at it.
.Fn dq_event_read
also accepts a
.Dv DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
record and
.Fn dq_event_write
a
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC
one, which have those bodies.
.Fn dq_event_write
and
.Fn dq_event_ioctl
also check that the payload the body's length field counts
.Pq Va dw_len , dio_len
lies within the record, and return it in
.Fa *data
or
.Fa *in
.Pq Dv NULL No for none
and
.Fa *len ,
without the padding.
.Fn dq_event_reply_record
does the same for an observer's
.Dv DQ_OP_REPLY
record:
.Fa *len
is the reply's length and
.Fa *data
its bytes, or
.Dv NULL
when the record carries none
.Pq Dv DQ_EVENT_F_NODATA , or a reply without a body .
Each returns 0, or -1 with
.Er EINVAL
for a record of another operation or one too short for its body.
.Pp
.Fn dq_reply
answers the event
.Fa id
with
.Fa error
(0 or an errno) and the body
.Fa data
of
.Fa len
bytes.
For
.Dv DQ_OP_READ
the body is up to the asked length of file bytes and a short answer is
the end of the file; for
.Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
it is exactly one
.Vt struct dq_ident_file
when
.Fa error
is 0.
Any nonzero
.Fa error
takes an empty body.
The other bodies have their helpers:
.Fn dq_reply_write
answers a
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
with the count taken
.Pq at most Va dw_len ; less is a short write
and the offset the bytes went to (for
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_APPEND
the controller's end of file; the requester's implicit offset becomes
.Fa offset
+
.Fa written ) ;
.Fn dq_reply_fspacectl
answers a
.Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL
with the range left undone, whose
.Fa offset
is at least
.Va dsc_offset
and whose end is
.Va dsc_offset No + Va dsc_len
(a finished request answers that end and 0);
.Fn dq_reply_lock
answers a
.Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK
with the blocking lock, or
.Va dlr_type
.Dv F_UNLCK ;
.Fn dq_reply_ioctl
answers a
.Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
with
.Fa error
and
.Fa out Ns Bq Fa len ,
where
.Fa len
must be
.Fn IOCPARM_LEN dio_cmd
for a successful
.Dv IOC_OUT
command and 0 otherwise.
A successful
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR ,
.Dv DQ_OP_FSYNC ,
.Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE ,
other
.Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
operations, and every error, take no body:
.Fn dq_reply
with
.Fa len
0.
.Pp
.Er ESRCH
means the id is not in flight: unknown, already answered, abandoned by an
interrupted or timed-out requester, or revoked (counted in
.Va ds_late_replies ) .
.Er EINVAL
is returned for a body the operation does not take (longer than asked,
of the wrong size, present with an error, or breaking the operation's rule
in
.Xr dq 4 ) ,
a malformed identity, an errno outside the kernel's range, a length above
.Va kern.dq.max_io ,
or a reply to an event that takes none; the event then stays in flight and
can be answered again.
.Er EFAULT
for an unreadable
.Fa data .
.Pp
The errno a controller may answer is allow-listed per operation; any other
reaches the requester as
.Er EIO
and is counted in
.Va ds2_bad_replies .
.Fn dq_reply_errno_allowed
returns nonzero when
.Fa error
reaches the requester as itself:
