.Fn dq_dispatch
performs one
.Fn dq_read_events
into a buffer sized for the largest record the kernel can post and calls
.Fa handler
once per record with the header, the body and
.Fa arg .
The handler returns 0 to continue or nonzero to stop.
.Fn dq_dispatch
returns the number of records handed to the handler, or -1 with the errno
of the read.
Records needing no answer
.Pq Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE , DQ_OP_INTERRUPT , DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL
carry
.Va de_id
0.
.Ss Resolution
.Fn dq_attach_starts
is
.Fn dq_attach
with served starts: when not -1,
.Fa rootfd
and
.Fa cwdfd
are served directories of
.Fa q
the caller holds, which become the process's served root and working
directory (stage 1c-iii-c; see
.Xr dq 4 ) .
The attachment shares their
.Vt struct file
with the caller's descriptors, which the caller may close.
Without
.Fa cwdfd
the working directory is the root; a
.Fa cwdfd
must be under the root
.Pq Er EINVAL .
With a root the child's open directories follow
.Xr chroot 2 Ns 's
rule exactly
.Pq Va kern.chroot_allow_open_directories , Er EPERM :
with the default of 1 a child that is not chrooted keeps its open real
directories, usable as real lookup starts.
For confinement,
.Fn dq_attach_starts_flags
with
.Dv DQ_ATTACH_F_NODIRS
refuses a child with any real directory open
.Pq Er EBUSY ,
with or without a root.
.Pp
.Fn dq_attach
attaches the process named by the process descriptor
.Fa procfd
to the queue
.Pq Dv DQ_IOC_ATTACH :
from then on every name it, and every process it forks, resolves, and
every
.Xr socket 2
and
.Xr socketpair 2
call they make, is posted as a
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
event and waits for the answer.
The target is typically a
.Xr pdfork 2 Ns ed
child attached before it execs: it must not have exec'd, share its
descriptor table or working-directory state, hold a descriptor of the
queue (which
.Fn dq_open
opens close-on-fork), or be attached already, or the call fails with
.Er EBUSY ;
an exited child is
.Er ESRCH ,
anything but a process descriptor
.Er EINVAL .
An attached process gains no privilege; see
.Xr dq 4 .
.Pp
.Fn dq_event_resolve
checks a
.Dv DQ_OP_RESOLVE
record and returns its fixed body (112 bytes) and its first four strings:
the path, the second path of a rename or link, the start hint and the
second path's start hint, each
NUL-terminated, possibly empty, and checked to end where its length
says inside the record.
.Fn dq_event_resolve_root
returns the fifth, the process's served root: its object
.Pq 0 No with a real root
and presented path.
Any output pointer may be
.Dv NULL .
It fails with
.Er EINVAL
for another operation or a malformed record.
A
.Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKET
or
.Dv DQ_ROP_SOCKETPAIR
body has empty strings, start kind
.Dv DQ_START_NONE ,
and the request in
.Va drs_domain ,
.Va drs_type
(without the
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv SOCK_CLOFORK
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK
flags)
and
.Va drs_protocol
(the protocol of the protocol switch the kernel found; for a switch whose
own protocol is 0, such as raw IP,
.Dv AF_UNIX
or
.Dv PF_ROUTE ,
the caller's number as given and not validated).
.Pp
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_pass
answers
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS :
the lookup proceeds unchanged.
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_errno
answers an errno, which becomes the system call's result; an errno
outside the operation's list
.Pq Fn dq_resolve_errno_allowed
reaches the requester as
.Er EIO .
Every lookup must be answered: an unanswered one fails with
.Er EIO
at its deadline, and when the queue closes every lookup of an attached
process fails with
.Er EIO ,
every
.Xr socket 2
and
.Xr socketpair 2
with
.Er EACCES .
.Pp
.Pa /usr/share/examples/dq/dqpass.c
runs a command attached and answers every lookup
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS .
.Ss Served answers
From stage 1c-iii-a a lookup of an attached process may be answered with
a served result, of a kind the event accepts:
.Fn dq_resolve_accepts
tests
.Va drs_accept
for one
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_*
kind (see
.Xr dq 4
for which operations and lookups take which kinds);
.Fn dq_resolve_errno_allowed_ev
is the errno allow-list of this event
.Er ( ENOTCAPABLE
only for a
.Dv DQ_RF_STRICTREL ,
.Dv DQ_RF_BENEATH
or
.Dv DQ_RF_RINROOT
lookup).
.Fn dq_event_resolve_ext
is
.Fn dq_event_resolve
that also returns the
.Vt struct dq_setattr_body
of a
.Dv DQ_ROP_SETATTR
or
.Dv DQ_ROP_TRUNCATE
event (else
.Dv NULL ) .
.Pp
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_fd
answers with a served object minted at the reply: kind
.Dv DQ_KIND_FILE ,
flags
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_WRITE
(the requester's descriptor may write; needs
.Va dp_write
=
.Dv DQ_EVENT )
and
.Dv DQ_MINT_F_CREATED
(this
.Dv O_CREAT
open created the file; the kernel skips its access check).
.Fa *objectp
receives the id before the object is linked and is undefined when the
reply fails;
.Fa reasonp ,
which may be
.Dv NULL ,
receives the
.Vt enum dq_reason
of an
.Er EINVAL .
Every id a successful reply returned ends exactly once, with
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
when the policy subscribes, also when the requester could not use the
answer.
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_attr
answers attributes only (stat, access, realpath; any file type).
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_link
answers
.Xr readlink 2
with an
.Dv S_IFLNK
identity and the target (its
.Va di_size
must be
.Fn strlen target ;
the target may be empty, as
.Xr symlink 2
allows).
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_ok
says the controller performed a mutating operation.
.Pp
.Fn dq_tree_create
prepares
.Fn dq_serve_tree ,
the served-tree controller in its stage 1c-iii-a mode: the directory
open at
.Fa rootfd
(the backing tree) is presented at the real-namespace directory
.Fa prefix .
A lookup whose path, made absolute with the event's start hint and
normalized, lies under
.Fa prefix
is answered from the backing tree, symbolic links there followed as
.Xr namei 9
would: an open of a regular file with
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
.Dv ( O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_EXCL
performed by the tree,
.Dv O_TRUNC
by the kernel through the object), stat, access and realpath with
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_ATTR ,
readlink with
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_LINK ,
chmod, chown, utimes, chflags and truncate with
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
of the target, the creating and removing operations with
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_OK
after performing them (a rename or link across
.Fa prefix
is
.Er EXDEV ) ,
exec
.Er EACCES ,
extended attributes and ACLs
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Every other lookup is passed: names outside
.Fa prefix ,
directories and the other non-regular nodes, the kernel's own lookups,
and every event whose accept set lacks the kind.
A served object's
.Va di_path
is the real-namespace name the process resolved, never the backing
path, and its
.Va ds_mntonname
is
.Ql / .
A symbolic link leading out of
.Fa prefix
is
.Er EACCES
in this mode.
Object events go to
.Fn dq_serve_file
on the backing file;
.Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE
closes it.
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_WRITE
serves writes; without it every creation, removal, rename, link,
truncation and write open is
.Er EROFS .
Any answer the kernel refuses (other than a vanished event) or that
cannot be built is followed by
.Er EIO ,
so no event is left in flight.
Renames honour
.Dv AT_RENAME_NOREPLACE
.Pq Va drs_mode .
.Fn dq_tree_objects
counts the objects the tree holds.
.Ss Served directories and REAL
Stage 1c-iii-b serves directories and symbolic links.
.Fn dq_mint_dir
mints a
.Dv DQ_KIND_DIR
object as
.Fn dq_mint_file
mints a file; a child that inherits the descriptor and is attached to
the queue uses it as a lookup start (a lookup from it is posted with
.Dv DQ_START_SERVED
and its object in
.Va drs_start ,
and never takes
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS ) ,
an unattached process gets
.Er EACCES
and one attached to another queue
.Er EDEADLK .
.Fn dq_policy_default_dir
fills the policy a directory or a symbolic link admits.
.Fn dq_ident_from_lstat
fills an identity from
.Fa name
relative to
.Fa dirfd
without following a final symbolic link, presented as
.Fa presented .
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_fd
also answers with
.Dv DQ_KIND_DIR ;
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_fd_symlink
answers with a served symbolic link carrying
.Fa target
(its
.Va di_size
becomes the target's length), for an
.Dv O_PATH | O_NOFOLLOW
open or an operation that does not follow the link.
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_real
answers that the name is the real absolute normalized
.Fa path ;
the lookup continues there.
.Pp
.Fn dq_reply_resolve_swap
answers an open that accepts
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_SWAP
with
.Fa fd ,
a descriptor of the controller
.Pq descriptor substitution :
the kernel installs that open file in the
requester as the result of its
.Xr open 2 ,
and the requester then works on the real file with no further events
.Po
.Xr dq 4 ,
.Sx Descriptor substitution ,
which also lists the duties of a controller that answers this way
.Pc .
Open
.Fa fd
afresh for each answer, with the access mode and status flags of the
request
.Pq Va drs_mode ,
without
.Dv O_TRUNC
and with
.Dv O_CLOFORK ,
and close it after the call returns 0; the call never closes it.
.Fa flags
is 0 or a combination of
.Dv DQ_SWAP_F_NOXFER
and
.Dv DQ_SWAP_F_CREATED .
The kernel's error is returned unchanged:
.Er EBADF , EOPNOTSUPP , EFTYPE , ENOTCAPABLE , EBUSY
and
.Er EINVAL
refuse
.Fa fd
or the reply and leave the event in flight, so another answer may follow;
.Er ESRCH
means the requester is gone.
.Er EBUSY
(the open file has another reference, possibly a transient one taken by
another thread) is retried once before it is returned.
.Pp
The file is handed over as it is: it keeps the credential of the
controller's
.Xr open 2
and the rights of
.Fa fd ,
also when the controller is another user.
A file opened by root keeps set-ID bits on write, may fill the reserved
blocks and is not held to a quota in the requester's hands; the kernel
does not prevent that.
A controller therefore confines itself before it answers:
.Fn dq_open ,
.Fn dq_attach ,
then
.Xr setuid 2
to an unprivileged user
.Po
and
.Xr jail_attach 2
or
.Xr cap_enter 2
where that fits
.Pc ,
and only then the
.Xr open 2
of each file it hands over.
Every function of this library except
.Fn dq_open
and
.Fn dq_observe_connect ,
which open a device by path, works after that.
In capability mode the library cannot read the sysctls it consults and
assumes their default values:
.Va kern.dq.max_io
for the buffer of
.Fn dq_dispatch ,
and
.Va vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot
and
.Va vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot_nonlocal
for the served trees.
The order, and what each confinement bounds, is in
.Xr dq 4 ,
.Sx The substituted file and the confined controller .
.Pp
.Fn dq_event_readdir
returns a
.Dv DQ_OP_READDIR
body: the cookie (the descriptor's offset) and the space the caller
left.
.Fn dq_dirent_add
appends a
.Vt struct dirent
record to
.Fa buf
.Po
.Fa cap
bytes,
.Fa *lenp
used;
.Er ENOSPC
when it does not fit
.Pc .
.Fn dq_reply_readdir
answers the records, the cookie after them and whether the directory
ended.
.Fn dq_serve_file
answers
.Dv DQ_OP_READDIR
by
.Xr getdirentries 2
of the backing directory from the cookie, which is its own offset, and
applies a
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
of an
.Dv O_PATH
backing descriptor (a symbolic link) with the
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
forms of
.Xr fchmodat 2
and friends.
.Pp
.Fn dq_tree_create
with
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_SERVED
selects the stage 1c-iii-b mode, and
.Fn dq_tree_root
then mints the tree's root as a served directory presented at
.Fa prefix
and returns the controller's descriptor of it.
In this mode directories are served (an open is answered with a
.Dv DQ_KIND_DIR
object, its entries read from the backing directory), a final symbolic
link opened
.Dv O_PATH | O_NOFOLLOW
is a served symbolic link (opened
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
alone it is
.Er EMLINK ) ,
and device, FIFO and socket nodes, AF_UNIX connects and binds, names
outside
.Fa prefix
(also reached through a backing symbolic link) and the operations the
tree does not serve are answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
with the real path (the backing node's for names under
.Fa prefix ) ,
when the event accepts it;
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
from a real start, and
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
under a restricted lookup, otherwise.
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_EXEC_REAL
answers exec, and an
.Dv O_EXEC
open of a regular file (for
.Xr fexecve 2 ) ,
with
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
of the backing binary.
.Pp
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_ROOT
(with
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_SERVED ,
stage 1c-iii-c) serves the tree as the attached processes' root, given
by
.Fn dq_attach_starts
with the root from
.Fn dq_tree_root ,
or a directory under it that the process chroots to.
A name from a served start is walked inside the tree.
.Xr chdir 2
and
.Xr chroot 2
of a directory are answered
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_FD
of a served directory
.Pq Er ENOTDIR No for anything else ,
and the interpreter's consultation at the served root
.Pq Dv DQ_ROP_EXEC_INTERP
.Dv DQ_RESOLVE_REAL
of the tree's own interpreter.
The event carries the process's root
.Pq Fn dq_event_resolve_root ,
and the tree resolves absolute names and links and
.Ql ..
at that root, so a process chrooted to a directory inside the tree sees
what
.Xr chroot 2
of the backing directory shows.
A served start whose hint is missing or not the object's own path is
answered
.Er EIO .
A real-start lookup of a name outside the tree is
.Er EACCES ;
with
.Dv DQ_TREE_F_OUTSIDE_PASS
it is passed
.Pq Dv DQ_RESOLVE_PASS
instead.
Paths are resolved as
.Xr namei 9
resolves them: component by component, through the backing tree inside
.Fa prefix
(every access confined beneath
.Fa rootfd
with
.Dv O_RESOLVE_BENEATH ) ,
canonical with
.Xr realpath 3
outside it, and under the lookup's restrictions
.Pq Dv DQ_RF_STRICTREL , DQ_RF_BENEATH , DQ_RF_RINROOT , No and the second path's Dv DQ_RF_STRICTREL2 , DQ_RF_BENEATH2 :
a
.Ql ..
above the start, an absolute name or symbolic link target, and any
.Ql ..
under capability mode while
.Va vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot
is 0 (or, from a non-local file system, while
.Va vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot_nonlocal
is 0), are
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
.Pq Dv RINROOT No clamps at the start instead .
.Ss Serving a backing file
.Fn dq_serve_file
answers one event from a real file open on
.Fa backing_fd ,
so a controller that serves a file it holds, with or without changes of
its own in a handler around it, need not write the operations itself.
An answer the kernel refuses, or one that cannot be built, is followed by
.Er EIO
(the call still returns the failure), so no waited event is left in
flight until its deadline:
.Bl -tag -width "DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL" -offset indent
.It Dv DQ_OP_READ , DQ_OP_READ_ASYNC
.Xr pread 2 ;
a short read is the end of the file.
The presented size must not exceed the backing file's: a reply short of
bytes the identity says exist fails a fault with
.Dv SIGBUS
and a
.Dv DQ_CACHE_DATA
read with
.Er EIO
.Pq Xr dq 4 .
.It Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2
and
.Xr fpathconf 2
of the backing file through
.Fn dq_ident_from_stat ,
keeping the presented path; needs
.Va so_ident ,
else
.Er EIO .
.It Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
.Xr pwrite 2
at
.Va dw_offset ,
or at the backing file's end under
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_APPEND ,
reported through
.Fn dq_reply_write ;
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_SYNC
and
.Dv DQ_WRITE_F_DSYNC
are followed by
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Xr fdatasync 2 .
.It Dv DQ_OP_WRITE_ASYNC
.Xr pwrite 2
of the dirty pages at
.Va dw_offset ,
answered with that offset; a backing errno outside the
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE
list (such as
.Er EPIPE )
is sent as
.Er EIO .
The identity copy is not refreshed after a write-back: the size and
times of a mapping are the kernel's to present.
.It Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR
the masked fields only, in the order that lets them all apply:
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Xr futimens 2
.Pq Dv UTIME_OMIT No for a time not in the mask ,
.Xr fchflags 2
last (an immutable flag set first would refuse the rest).
The birth time has no setter; the kernel applied it to the identity.
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSYNC
.Xr fdatasync 2
for
.Dv DQ_FSYNC_DATA ,
.Xr fsync 2
otherwise.
.It Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE
.Xr posix_fallocate 2 .
.It Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL
.Xr fspacectl 2 ,
answering the range it left.
.It Dv DQ_OP_LOCK
each owner gets its own open of the backing file
.Po
.Xr openat 2
with
.Dv O_EMPTY_PATH ,
close-on-exec and close-on-fork, so a forked requester never holds a copy
.Pc ,
read-write where allowed, on which every kind is applied as an OFD lock
.Pq Dv F_OFD_SETLK , F_OFD_GETLK :
the backing file's lock table then decides between owners as it would
between processes.
A flock or OFD owner is an open file, so it is one object's
.Pq Va de_object , dl_kind , dl_owner .
A POSIX owner is a process, which holds one set of locks per file however
many opens it has, so it is shared by every object of the same identity
.Pq Va di_dev , di_ino No of Va so_ident , or of the backing file without one
on the queue: one process never conflicts with itself through two
objects of one file, and an
.Dv DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL
of its POSIX locks from any of them releases them all.
Only
.Dv DQ_LOCK_SETLK
and
.Dv DQ_LOCK_SETLKW
create an owner; a
.Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK
by an owner that holds nothing asks through a temporary open closed at
once, and an
.Dv DQ_LOCK_UNLCK
by one is answered 0.
A
.Dv DQ_LOCK_SETLKW
blocks in a thread of its own, which answers when the lock is granted;
when that reply finds the event no longer in flight
.Pq Er ESRCH : No the requester gave up and its Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT No raced the grant
the grant is undone by unlocking the requested range for the owner,
which also drops what the owner held in that range before (an abandoned
upgrade leaves the range unlocked, where a real file would keep the old
lock).
Without
.Va so_state
every lock is
.Er ENOLCK .
A
.Dv DQ_LOCK_GETLK
answer carries
.Va dlr_pid
-1: the routine does not track which owner holds the blocker (a declared
difference from a real file, which names a POSIX owner's pid).
.It Dv DQ_OP_UNLOCK_ALL
closes that owner's open, which releases its locks, and cancels its
blocked waits.
.It Dv DQ_OP_IOCTL
the
.Va so_ioctl
