.Pc ;
.Va so_ident ,
the controller's copy of the identity, refreshed from the backing file
and sent with
.Fn dq_set_ident
after every successful
.Dv DQ_OP_SETATTR ,
and, under
.Dv DQ_SERVE_F_SYNC_IDENT
in
.Va so_flags ,
after every
.Dv DQ_OP_WRITE ,
.Dv DQ_OP_FALLOCATE
and
.Dv DQ_OP_FSPACECTL ,
so that the presented times and block count are the backing file's;
.Va so_ioctl
and its
.Va so_arg ;
.Va so_resolve
and its
.Va so_resolve_arg ;
and
.Va so_flags :
.Dv DQ_SERVE_F_SYNC_IDENT
and
.Dv DQ_SERVE_F_RESOLVE_STRICT .
.Fn dq_serve_file
returns 0 once the record is answered or needed no answer, and -1 with
the errno of the reply when that failed.
.Fa body
and
.Fa bodylen
are those
.Fn dq_dispatch
hands a handler, accepted so the routine is a handler's tail call; the
record itself is what is read.
.Ss The dq.observe service
.Fn dq_observe_connect
opens
.Pa /dev/mac_capability ,
connects to the
.Dq dq.observe
service and returns the service descriptor; the device is closed again.
The service admits a credential that may open
.Pa /dev/dq .
.Pp
.Fn dq_observe_list_queues
lists the queues of the system starting at index
.Fa first
into
.Fa buf Ns Bq Fa nbuf
and stores how many were filled in
.Fa *count
and how many exist in
.Fa *total
(either may be
.Dv NULL ) ;
.Fn dq_observe_list_objects
does the same for the objects of
.Fa queue ,
identified by its
.Va ds_queue_id .
A caller pages by advancing
.Fa first .
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown queue,
.Er EPROTO
for a malformed reply.
.Pp
.Fn dq_observe_subscribe
returns a metadata-only observer descriptor of
.Fa queue ,
as
.Fn dq_mint_observer
without
.Dv DQ_OBS_F_DATA
would: the service installs it in the caller itself, rights-limited and
locked like an ioctl-minted observer, and names it in a
.Vt struct dqo_subscribe_reply .
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown queue,
.Er ENXIO
for a closing one,
.Er EPROTO
for a malformed reply.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dq_open ,
.Fn dq_mint_observer ,
.Fn dq_observe_connect
and
.Fn dq_observe_subscribe
return a descriptor, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Fn dq_dispatch
returns a record count, or -1.
.Fn dq_event_first ,
.Fn dq_event_next
and
.Fn dq_event_body
return pointers into the caller's buffer.
.Fn dq_reply_errno_allowed
and
.Fn dq_resolve_errno_allowed
return nonzero or 0.
.Fn dq_serve_state_create
returns a state, or
.Dv NULL
with
.Va errno
set;
.Fn dq_serve_state_owners
returns a count.
.Fn dq_policy_default
and
.Fn dq_serve_state_destroy
return nothing.
The other functions return 0 on success and -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A controller that serves a file's bytes from memory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int q, fd, reason;
uint64_t object;
struct dq_policy pol;
struct dq_ident_file ident;

q = dq_open();
dq_policy_default(&pol);
dq_ident_from_stat(&ident, path, &st, &sfs, realfd);
if (dq_mint_file(q, &ident, &pol, &object, &fd, &reason) == -1)
	err(1, "mint (reason %d)", reason);
/* fd is inherited by a fork(2)ed requester; the queue is not. */
for (;;)
	if (dq_dispatch(q, handler, &served) == -1)
		err(1, "dq_dispatch");
.Ed
.Pp
where the handler answers a read from the buffer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
handler(int q, const struct dq_event *ev, const void *body,
    size_t bodylen, void *arg)
{
	const struct dq_read_body *rb;
	struct served *s = arg;
	size_t n;

	switch (ev->de_op) {
	case DQ_OP_READ:
		rb = body;
		n = rb->dr_offset >= s->len ? 0 :
		    MIN(rb->dr_len, s->len - rb->dr_offset);
		return (dq_reply(q, ev->de_id, 0, s->data + rb->dr_offset,
		    n) == -1);
	case DQ_OP_CLOSE:
		s->closed = true;
		return (0);
	default:
		return (0);
	}
}
.Ed
.Pp
A controller that serves a real file writable, every event answered from
it by
.Fn dq_serve_file :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct dq_serve_opts opts = { 0 };

backing = open(path, O_RDWR);
dq_policy_default(&pol);
pol.dp_write = DQ_EVENT;
pol.dp_events = DQ_EVENTS_CLOSE;
opts.so_state = dq_serve_state_create();
opts.so_ident = &ident;
if (dq_mint_file_flags(q, &ident, &pol, DQ_MINT_F_WRITE, &object,
    &fd, &reason) == -1)
	err(1, "mint (reason %d)", reason);

static int
serve(int q, const struct dq_event *ev, const void *body,
    size_t bodylen, void *arg)
{
	if (dq_serve_file(q, ev, body, bodylen, backing, &opts) == -1)
		warn("dq_serve_file");
	return (ev->de_op == DQ_OP_CLOSE);
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Pa /usr/share/examples/dq/dqserve.c
is a complete controller: a read-only redacting server, and with
.Fl w
a writable one, serving a shared writable mapping too, built on
.Fn dq_serve_file .
